A||||13||Shaun41178||Dyno Runs 
Z||000000||Shaun41178||07-28-99||03:47 PM||||I got some dyno runs in today.  Two to be exact.  One with nitrous and one with out.  Today I ran with a 50 shot instead of my normal 70 shot<p>Just for some info the last time I had my car dynoes I peaked at 128 hp with out the nitrous and with a misfire above 3500 rpm.  I went back today and with ported exhaust manifolds and K&N custom intake of my design and peaked at 138 hp.  I was shy of where I wanted to be which was 140-145 peak hp.<p>I then did a dyno run with my nitrous and peaked at 160 hp.  So I only really picked up about 22 hp according to the two printouts. <p>There are several ways that the computer measures the hp.  Since I have an auto it measures all the peaks and valleys and gives the average of that power and puts it in a power curve.  Doing it this way I peaked at 110 hp.  However with the nitrous I peaked at 154 hp.  So a 44 hp jump with a 50 shot of nitrous is good.  Peak torque with this same method was 214 lb-ft.  Which is about where it should be.  That is up from 157 last time I had it dynoed.  So with a 50 hp shot of nitrous I picked up around 60 lb ft of torque.  The peak torque today was at 148.  That is down from the 157 before the ported manifolds and intake work but the nitrous more then makes up for the loss.  With my 70 shot I am guessing I have about another 15-20 lb-ft of torque which would put me around 230 to the wheels which is about 250-260 lb-ft of torque at the crank.  DAMN that is a lot of torque for this small car.  But it feels great.  I need to get an air/fuel guage so I can see if I am leaning out on the top end cause I think I am.  I just might need larger injectors<p>If you want to see my old dyno charts they will be up for a couple more days.  my website is http://members.aol.com/shaun41178
Z||000001||ChadMan||07-29-99||07:44 AM||||160 hp at the rear wheels is really impressive.  That's probably right around 200 net hp at the crank!<p>There's a really good article on www.caranddriver.com about chassis dynos (it's under Opinions/Brock Yates/Dynojet).  They guesstimate "a power loss of about 18 percent for a manual-transmission drivetrain and up to 20 percent for an automatic between the engine flywheel and the drive wheels."
Z||000002||FieroMike||07-29-99||07:52 AM||||Nice... good job Shaun.  What are your mods?
Z||000003||Shaun41178||07-29-99||02:04 PM||||Actually the guy at the dyno house said that he has found that there is around a 23-26% loss from the engine to the wheels on an automatic transmission.  Whether or not mine is losing 23 percent or not I don't know but it seems almost about right.so if the engien made 140 hp you would have around 110 at the wheels.<p>When averageing out all the peaks and valleys in my dyno run it comes out to 110 at the wheels but I peaked at 138hp.  Since I have a auto instead of a manual I have to run through all the gears and when the gears change I get a big spike of hp for a half second and this is where this peak hp occured.  Running through all three gears I only hit 100 hp at the wheels, in second it got up to 110hp, and third got me up to around 117hp or so.  <p>When I hit third gear it seemed like I had a bog or something occur in the tranny.  The car shifted to third and a second later it felt like it bogged down and then slowly accelerated.  I wonder if this was the torque converter locking up or something.  Can the torque converter still lock up under WOT?  If this is what happened which I am sure it did, then it definately cost me some power. <p>My car was run up to 5500 rpm in each gear and 5500 in third gear got me up to 128 mph.  So I guess that with a 3.06 final drive and a v6 the cars top speed is 128 mph.  That is of course without wind resistance and rolling reasistance but none the less it has the gearing to go that fast.  With wind resistance and rolling resistance I would say I am limited to about 120mph<p>My mods consist of an engine that has around 32k miles on it that just has a stock rebuild.  It has a TB bypass Crane HI-6 ignition and PS91 coil.  Accel 8.8mm 150 ohms per foot wires, stock plugs, custom intake of my design with K&N cone filter, ported echaust manifolds and then a 50 hp shot of nitrous(I normally run 70 on the street but at the dyno I ran a 50 just to be safe so add  some more hp and tq for the 70 shot)  That is it as far as mods go.  The ported manifolds and the intake netted me about 10 rwhp.  Last time I went I peaked at 128 and this time i peaked at 138.  I did lose 7 peak pound feet of low end torque however which is due to the intake mod and the ported exhaust manifolds but that loss was made up with the increase in hp.<p>I went to Milan dragway last night and did some drag racing(only got to race twice)  My first run I let the engine cool and ran without nitrous and hit a 16.7 at 81mph.  Way slow but it was also very hot out and humid and everyone was running slow all day.  Mustangs could barely get into the 14's and these were the new ones too.  So then I got back to the staging lanes dropped some ice on the intake for about 5 minutes and also disconnected my torque converter plug that goes into the valve body just to see if it makes a difference.  And by God I think it did.  I had my nitrous on as well for this run and jetted it with a 50 hp increase.  I clicked off a 14.9 at 90mph.  With a 50 hp shot of nitrous I took 1.8 seconds off of my et and I gained 9 mph.  I think that when the tranny hits 3rd gear the torque converter locks up and almost keeps the car from accelerating in third like it should.  Just a reminder I went to the strip several months ago and ran a 70 shot on a cooler day then yesterday and I ran a best of 14.8.  That was with the torque converter still plugged in.  So I think that by disconnecting the switch to the TC you can get a couple of tenths in the qtr.  I mean because a 50 shot just can't do that by itself espescially since my 70 shot barely got me to 14.8 but mostly ran 14.9 and now with my 50 I ran a 14.9.  If I had one more run last night I would've put in the 70 shot and see what I woud've run.  I think I could have hit 14.7. i definately have a mid 14 second car now I just have to get it tuned now.  <p>So poeple with auto tranny's and going drag racing, disconnect the TCC solenoid on the outside of the valve body.  It should make your car faster and prevent the TC from locking up.
Z||000004||sid1200||07-29-99||06:13 PM||||you mention a stock auto having 110 hp at the wheels, what about a stock 5sp?  anyone know?
Z||000005||sid1200||07-29-99||06:20 PM||||you mention a stock auto having 110 hp at the wheels, what about a stock 5sp?  anyone know?
Z||000006||SCCA FIERO||07-29-99||06:55 PM||||Shaun, have you consiredered putting a manual tranny in that beast? <p>Also...I had an 86SE V6(in Fiero heavan now) and it had the 4speed. I then purchased an 88 coupe and installed the V6 from the 86. The 88 coupe 5 speed is retained(Isuzu). This car is fast, but my 86 with the 4speed seemed to be much faster. It is prolly because of the gearing, but I am thinking of putting the 4speed back in it. I have also a 5speed Getrag that I acquired a little while ago. That may be my actual choice. My concern is if I don't like the Getrag then it all comes out again. <p>During the swap I installed the wrong clutch (DOH!) and the cradle was dropped twice in about a week (not to mention dropping the 86 cradle too) I am getting very good at dropping them :) Took me and a buddy 2 hours to drop it the 2nd time.<p>WOW I sure can ramble on....anyway, with all the time and money you put into your Fiero, wouldn't a 5 or 4 speed be a very worthy upgrade?
Z||000007||Shaun41178||07-29-99||07:48 PM||||I am not at all interested in installing a manual tranny in my Fiero.  I think that it is just too mush of a hassle to get a little bit quicker times in the qtr.  besides I have a 3.33 tranny in my garage that I am going to install with a shift kit.  I am sure that will take a couple of tenths off the qtr mile time. <p>Someone on the Fiero List said that he and his girl raced each other.  the car he was driving had the 4.10 4spd v6 and the car his girlfriend was driving had the 3.33 auto v6.  He said tha the was ahead of his girlfriend all the way to about 75-80 and that is when he got passed.  So it seems to me that the 3.33 auto is faster then the 4.10 4spd in the qtr mile.  <p>Also a manual tranny car is only faster if the guy driving knows how to shift properly and at the right rpm and can shift quickly.  I never have to worry about missing a shift in my car or bogging the engine down or anything like that.  I just put my foot down and it does all the work for me.  With a shift kit my tranny will shift faster then any manual tranny can.  Besides all the quickest drag cars in the world use a auto tranny cause it shift faster and is less dependant on the driver.<p>I am promising myself that by next summer I will have a sub 14 second car in the qtr.  <p>If anyone is interested I have found a 4.10 tranny here in Toledo Oh.  If I remember correctly I think the j-yard said that he would let everything go all the way down to the clutch pedal for $125.  That includes axles and cables.  Everything.  Just thought I would post this too.
Z||000008||Pretender||07-30-99||03:26 AM||||where is the torque c plug located again???...when i race at the local strip i start out in first and shift to second and keep it in second the whole way down the track. when i cross the line i'm near if not at redline, i figure once i get the ing.(same as yours) i'll have nothing to worry about up high. my best is a 16.2 and a worst of 17.7..the heat really does make a difference!, also do you have any problems hooking up when your N20 kicks in?..
Z||000009||Shaun41178||07-30-99||04:23 AM||||Pretender: the torque converter solenoid plug that I am talking about is located on the valve body of the transmission on the firewall(front side) of the engine also near the crossover pipe O2 sensor.  It is a blue colored connector that connects into the valve body/bellhousing.  It is quite hard to miss if you are lookin for it.  When going to run at the strip, do one run with it connected and if possible get right back in the staging lanes and disconnect the plug from the valve body and see if on your next run you are quicker.  I bet it will be.  A 16.2 is good time for an auto tranny fiero.  how many miles on yours and what mods you got?  You might break into the 15's with the solenoid disconnected.<p>huh that is wierd that your car can hit the qtr in second gear.  My tranny shifts at 5500 rpm every time and it has to shift before it gets to the end of the track.  My tranny shifts from second to third at 74 mph.  I cross the finish line with my nitrous at 90 mph.  I will be well over redline if I left the car in second.<p>I have no problems getting traction when activating my nitrous when i am at the track.  At the dragstrip they put down VHT which is a chemical which helps the track get stickier to aid the tires in gripping the track.  I punch the nitrous right off the line of wait a half second and then punch the nitrous.  I have gotten a few squeaks from the tires but I hook really well.  That is with 33 psi in the back tires too.  If I lowered the pressure to like 28 or something I am sure I wouldn't lose traction at all at the strip.  On the street I have very minimal tire slippage when I hit the nitrous from a dead stop and none if I wait a second or two after launch.  with the transfer of weight on acceleration over the rear wheels it is hard for the tires to break loose.  If you have larger/wider tires then 215/60/14 like me, you probably won't lose traction at all.  I plan to get 245's on the rear soon.  That should just about eliminate the spin.<p>I think that any stock Fiero 2.8 in good running condition can handle a 50 shot of nitrous.  This was the best upgrade I made to my car.  The power increase is incredible and shows the potential of what the car could've been.  With the 50 shot of nitrous I had 214 peak torque at the wheels remember.  That is a butt load of torque for this car.  If you have been debating whether to get nitrous or not I say go for it.  You won't regret it.  Just don't get too hp crazy and jet it to 100 hp and you won't have to worry about blowing it up.
Z||000010||Pretender||07-31-99||12:41 AM||||I've got 84,000 on it so far. <p>ocelot exhaust(crappy in my opinion)<br>power pully,<br>ADS power chip,<br>wires and plugs(fiero store specials)<br>thermostat,<br>no spare tire/jack,pass. seat.<p>i ice down the manifold as much as i can. i also plan on having a 13 second street fiero before next year...i have 225-50-15 ZR's(BF goodwrenches in the rear) and ocelot cross drilled rotors in the front(pic's real soon). a simple air intake mod running without rear side vents alos helps. i am planning on buying the "stillen fuel pressure riser" on my next paycheck as well as a custom designied air intake(pvc pipe) and flexalite fans for the rear deck. i am going to install the 7) shot as soon as i get the extra money(will be before sept. 26)...have you thought about the "jacobs nitrous controller" from summit?..i guess since you have no tire slippage i don't have to think about that extra money..do you have any tips about installing the n2o?...have you finished your install on the ing.(web page that is.)?
Z||000011||Shaun41178||07-31-99||02:20 PM||||I too have just purchased a power pulley.  I haven't installed it yet so I don't know if I will be able to "feel" difference but I doubt that it will.  I will probably get a dyno test to see if it does add any power at all.  I also plan on getting pics and doing a write up on my webpage about the install of the pulley and the difference between the stock and power pulley.<p>Yes the article on my webpage on my nitrous install is complete.  I don't know if I can add much more info on my install but I have found some new links for some great nitrous info that I will have to add to my nitrous page.  Just go to my homepage at http://members.aol.com/shaun41178  and scroll down to nitrous install to see my nitrous page.<p>The Jacobs nitrous controller would be nice but I don't need it for my application and i don't think you will need it for yours either.  The Stillen fuel pressure riser is a good investment especially since you are running nitrous and is a good tuning tool.  On a stock engine I don't think the pressure riser will have any effect on hp.  I am basing this on someone who did extensive testing on the accel adjustable fuel pressure regulator and only got a 1-2 hp increase at the most at different psi's and they both work by the same principle too.  However with the nitrous I think it would be a good tuning device and a way to monitor fuel pressure under WOT on the nitrous.  If you need any help installing your nitrous system or just have any questions in general just ask and i will help as much as I can.<p>I will have pics of my custom intake system up on my webpage too with some flexible heat ducting and pvc tubing.  Mine looks great and stock too.  Just have to get the pics developed and then sacan them in.
Z||000012||DXR_WILL||08-02-99||01:23 PM||||Shaun41178/Pretender-Isn't Stillens Fuel Pressure Regulator expensive.  I don't know were you live.  I have one on my Celica for NOS as well and out here in California they are close to $200 and in some cases well over $200 bucks.  Can't you buy a cheaper regulator and still get the same effect?  
Z||000013||Pretender||08-03-99||03:33 AM||||your right about the stillen being expensive($300!)..but it is the best out on the market and i need to have the best on my car..i can't afford a mishap while shooting 70 extra hp's into my baby...but maybe if i see something better..my mind isn't made up completely but i'm thinking i will buy the stillen though...
